## Decoding the question
A **short note of 10 marks** on **two** organisations: about **550–650 words**. Give each its founding, aims, methods and achievements, then **compare** them — a table or compare figure is ideal — and end with their significance.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction
Pakistan's women's movement has worked through organisations as much as through individuals. The **All Pakistan Women's Association (APWA)** and the **Women's Action Forum (WAF)** are the two most important women's organisations in Pakistan's history. They represent two phases of the movement: **welfare and reform** after independence, and **rights and resistance** under military rule.

### 2. APWA (1949)

#### i) Founding
APWA was founded in ==1949== by **Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan**, wife of the first prime minister, who was also its **first president**. It grew out of women's **relief work** for refugees during Partition.

#### ii) Aims and work
A non-political **welfare** organisation, it set up **schools, colleges, vocational centres, clinics and cottage industries** for women across the country. Its branches spread to every province, and it gained consultative status with the United Nations.

#### iii) Advocacy
APWA also pressed for reform. After Prime Minister **Bogra** took a second wife (==1955==), APWA led protests that contributed to the **Rashid Commission** and the **Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961**, which regulated polygamy and divorce and required registration of marriages.

### 3. WAF (1981)

#### i) Founding
WAF was founded in **Karachi** in ==1981==, soon spreading to Lahore, Islamabad and Peshawar. It was formed after a court sentenced **Fehmida and Allah Bux** to stoning for *zina* under the **Hudood Ordinances** (==1979==).

#### ii) Character
A **non-hierarchical, non-party, secular** platform of women's groups and individuals, refusing donor funding to keep its independence. Decisions were taken collectively, with no president or permanent leader.

#### iii) Struggle
WAF challenged the **Hudood Ordinances**, the proposed **Law of Evidence**, and restrictions on women in media and sport. On **12 February 1983**, WAF members and women lawyers were **baton-charged** by police in Lahore — the day is now **National Women's Day**. WAF also documented Hudood cases such as that of **Safia Bibi** (==1983==), a young rape victim convicted of zina, whose case caused a national outcry.

### 4. APWA and WAF compared

::: table | APWA and WAF compared.
@widths 3,5.5,5.5
Point | APWA | WAF
Founded | ==1949==, by Begum Ra'ana Liaquat Ali Khan | ==1981==, Karachi, after the Fehmida–Allah Bux case
Era | Nation-building after independence | Zia-ul-Haq's martial law
Aim | Welfare, education, skills, health | Resist discriminatory laws; women's rights
Method | Service; cooperation with the state | Protest, lobbying, legal analysis, media
Politics | Non-political, reformist | Secular, non-party, confrontational
Achievement | Institutions for women; support for the MFLO 1961 | Resistance to Hudood laws; a rights movement
Criticism | Elite charity; avoided hard politics | Urban and middle-class; limited rural reach
:::

### 5. Legacy

#### i) What each gave
APWA showed that women could build **national institutions**, and made girls' education respectable. WAF turned women's issues into **political rights**, gave women a collective voice against a military ruler, and trained a generation of activists, lawyers and legislators whose work led to later laws — from the **Protection of Women Act 2006** to the honour-killing law of ==2016==.

#### ii) Shared limits
Both were led by **educated, urban women**; both struggled to reach rural and working women, who organised separately in movements such as the **Sindhiani Tehreek**.

#### iii) Conclusion
Their differences reflect their times: APWA worked with a friendly new state; WAF faced a hostile military regime. APWA and WAF are best seen as **two stages of one movement**: first **service**, then **resistance**. Today's Aurat March inherits WAF's spirit of protest and APWA's commitment to women's welfare.

## Why this answer scores
- Each organisation gets **founding, aims, methods and achievements** with exact dates.
- A **comparison table** answers the implicit "compare" in a two-part note.
- The **legacy** section links both to later laws and the Aurat March.
